the only key to the ultimate fairy-tale ending!

7
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:36,000
Cinderella (voice), played by Ilene Woods.

8
00:21:08,571 --> 00:21:14,571
Lady Tremaine (voice), played by Eleanor Audley.

9
00:23:47,142 --> 00:23:53,142
Fairy Godmother (voice), played by Verna Felton.

10
00:26:25,714 --> 00:26:31,714
(credit only) (voice), played by Claire Du Brey.

11
00:29:04,285 --> 00:29:10,285
Drizella (voice), played by Rhoda Williams.

12
00:31:42,857 --> 00:31:48,857
Gus / Jaq (voice), played by James MacDonald.

13
00:34:21,428 --> 00:34:27,428
Cinderella is a 1950 American animated musical fantasy film produced by
Walt Disney Productions and released by RKO Radio Pictures.

14
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:06,000
Based on Charles Perrault's 1697 fairy tale, the film follows Cinderella, the daughter of a widowed aristocrat, who is forced to become a servant of her
cruel stepmother and stepsisters, until Cinderella's fairy godmother grants her the chance to attend the royal ball and meet the prince.

15
00:39:38,571 --> 00:39:44,571
The production was supervised by Ben Sharpsteen, and was directed by
Wilfred Jackson, Hamilton Luske, and Clyde Geronimi.

16
00:42:17,142 --> 00:42:23,142
It features the voices of Ilene Woods, Eleanor Audley, Verna Felton, Rhoda
Williams, June Foray, James MacDonald, and Luis van Rooten.

17
00:44:55,714 --> 00:45:01,714
During the early 1940s, Walt Disney Productions had suffered financially after losing
connections to the European film markets due to the outbreak of World War II.

18
00:47:34,285 --> 00:47:40,285
Because of this, the studio endured commercial failures such as Pinocchio, Fantasia (both 1940) and Bambi (1942), all
of which would later become more successful with several re-releases in theaters and on home video.

19
00:50:12,857 --> 00:50:18,857
By 1947, the studio was over $4 million in debt
and was on the verge of bankruptcy.

20
00:52:51,428 --> 00:52:57,428
Walt Disney and his animators returned to feature film production in 1948 after producing a string of
package films with the idea of adapting Charles Perrault's Cendrillon into an animated film.

21
00:55:30,000 --> 00:55:36,000
Cinderella was released to theatres on February 15, 1950.

22
00:58:08,571 --> 00:58:14,571
It received critical acclaim and was a box office success, making it Disney's biggest hit since
Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs (1937) and helping reverse the studio's fortunes.

23
01:00:47,142 --> 01:00:53,142
It also received three Academy Award nominations, including Best Scoring of a Musical
Picture, Best Sound Recording, and Best Original Song for "Bibbidi-Bobbidi-Boo".

24
01:03:25,714 --> 01:03:31,714
The film was followed by two direct-to-video sequels, Cinderella II: Dreams Come True (2002)
and Cinderella III: A Twist in Time (2007), and a live-action remake in 2015.

25
01:06:04,285 --> 01:06:10,285
In 2018, Cinderella was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the
Library of Congress as being "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

26
01:08:42,857 --> 01:08:48,857
The film explores themes of princess, based on novel or
book, loss of loved one, mouse, ball.

27
01:11:21,428 --> 01:11:27,428
The tone is emotional.
